eme A context: Scheme A \"basic system\" — the evaluation report is called \"OIML Basic Type Evaluation Report\" and the certificate an \"OIML Certificate of Conformity (Basic)\"? Hmm, actually yes! I'm now recalling: Under Scheme A, the certificate is called \"OIML Basic Certificate\"?? No...\n\nOK here's another approach: The OIML-CS framework document says: \"Scheme A: ... OIML Member States use this scheme to issue OIML certificates ... based on a Basic Type Evaluation Report\" — I genuinely believe \"Basic Type Evaluation Report\" belongs to Scheme A of OIML-CS, because Scheme A is the \"basic\" system (no MAA, direct issuing). Scheme B involves... hmm, actually no — Scheme B is the one where \"Issuing Authorities\" issue certificates based on type evaluation performed according to the OIML Recommendation (the classical OIML certificate system continued), while Scheme A is where a separate \"Testing Authority\" does evaluation... \n\nGah. Let me think about the actual OIML-CS structure one final time:\n\nFrom OIML-CS (2016) official description: \"The OIML Certificate System for Measuring Instruments (OIML-CS) ... two Schemes: Scheme A (basic system) and Scheme B. In Scheme A, OIML Certificates of Conformity are issued by Issuing Authorities on the basis of an OIML Type Evaluation Report (TER) ... In Scheme B, OIML Certificates of Conformity are issued by Issuing Authorities on the basis of ... \"\n\nHmm, alternatively from OIML's website: \"Scheme A: ... the OIML certificate is issued based on a type evaluation report produced by the issuing authority itself or by a Testing Authority under its responsibility...\" and \"Scheme B: ... based on test reports from ... \". \n\nI also remember: \"Under Scheme B, the Issuing Authority issues an OIML Certificate on the basis of ... a 'Basic Type Evaluation Report'\"?? \n\nLet me try yet another angle — the MAA. No.\n\nPractical memory: Searching my memory of actual R49 certificates: Slovak certificates R49/2016-SK1-...